stly, it outlines the Mini Nutritional Assessment-Short Form (MNA-SF) criteria, which are used to evaluate nutritional status, thereby aiding in the identification of individuals at risk of malnutrition. Overall, TABLA 1 serves as a vital tool in the fields of nutrition and dietetics, offering a multifaceted approach to dietary management and assessment.\",22\r\n7812,ÁCIDOS GRASOS SATURADOS,\"ÁCIDOS GRASOS SATURADOS, or saturated fatty acids, are a type of fat that can significantly impact cholesterol levels and are often compared to unsaturated fats in dietary studies. These fats are typically recommended to be limited in a healthy diet due to their association with cardiovascular health. It is particularly important to restrict the intake of saturated fatty acids in children's diets to promote heart health. Additionally, ÁCIDOS GRASOS SATURADOS are included in nutritional information and are frequently evaluated in dietary research for their effects on health.\",6\r\n3234,TABLA 2,\"TABLA 2 is a comprehensive resource that encompasses a variety of dietary and nutritional information. It includes a table listing foods that are permitted in a broad low-sodium diet, as well as a reference to the composition of copper in different food groups. Additionally, TABLA 2 outlines steps proposed by Diego Gracia for a deliberative process in clinical nutrition.\n\nThe table also details foods that are restricted and allowed for individuals with phenylketonuria, providing essential guidance for dietary management in this condition. Furthermore, it presents various lipid solutions used for parenteral nutrition, including their composition and nutritional values, along with optional nutritional information per 100 g/100 ml.\n\nIn a broader context, TABLA 2 showcases data from a population study on body mass index (IMC) for men and women under 45 years of age. It also presents characteristics of different dietary assessment methods, clinical characteristics of various types of Maturity Onset Diabetes of the Young (MODY), including mutations, clinical features, and treatment options.\n\nMoreover, TABLA 2 provides an example recipe for enteral nutrition, detailing ingredient quantities necessary for formulation, and outlines the criteria for the Nutritional Risk Screening (NRS 2002) process. Overall, TABLA 2 serves as a multifaceted tool for understanding dietary needs and nutritional assessment across various contexts.\",13\r\n15830,DIEGO GRACIA,Diego Gracia is a professor who advocates for a deliberative process in clinical nutrition decision-making.,3\r\n7334,DIETA HIPOSÓDICA,\"The \"\"DIETA HIPOSÓDICA\"\" is a dietary regimen that restricts sodium intake, primarily recommended for patients with specific health conditions such as hypertension. This low-sodium diet aims to control sodium levels in the body, thereby reducing cardiovascular risks and improving arterial health. It is utilized to treat various health conditions by limiting the consumption of sodium, making it an essential dietary approach for individuals needing to manage their sodium intake for better health outcomes.\",15\r\n4918,TABLA 5,\"Table 5 presents a comprehensive overview of several key aspects related to patient care and nutrition. It includes the results of a study aimed at assessing the patient's hyperglycemic syndrome, providing valuable insights into this condition. Additionally, Table 5 summarizes the hydroelectrolytic content of various secretions, which is crucial for ensuring proper supplementation in parenteral nutrition. Furthermore, it outlines the mandatory and voluntary formats for presenting nutritional information, highlighting the importance of clear communication in dietary practices. Overall, Table 5 serves as an essential resource for understanding and managing nutritional needs in clinical settings.\",7\r\n15819,BEGOÑA ROMÁN,\"Begoña Román is a professor at the University of Barcelona, where she contributes to the academic community through her te